WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.288
Welcome to ID 553.

2
00:00:02.288 --> 00:00:05.975
ID 553 - Introduction Module.

3
00:00:05.975 --> 00:00:13.602
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:14.202 --> 00:00:19.716
Welcome to ID 553, Introduction to the Principles of
Instructional Design.

5
00:00:19.716 --> 00:00:21.058
I'm David Awoyemi.

6
00:00:21.058 --> 00:00:32.011
Instructional design is the systematic process of analyzing
learning needs, designing and developing instruction, and
evaluating whether it worked.

7
00:00:32.011 --> 00:00:42.442
Over eight modules we'll follow that arc, analysis, design,
development, implementation, and evaluation, while
questioning it along the way.

8
00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:51.980
You'll analyze two realistic design cases, and you'll
produce a complete instructional plan for a real problem of
your choosing.

9
00:00:52.580 --> 00:01:11.251
This course is for teachers who want to design instruction
more deliberately, for trainers and faculty developers who
build courses and workshops, for technologists who work
alongside subject-matter experts, and for graduate students
heading toward advanced coursework or research.

10
00:01:11.251 --> 00:01:14.706
No prior instructional design coursework is assumed.

11
00:01:14.706 --> 00:01:19.889
What I do assume is that you'll bring a real instructional
problem to work on.

12
00:01:20.489 --> 00:01:26.667
In module one you'll choose an instructional problem and
keep it for the whole course.

13
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:39.381
Good problems are real, bounded, and involve learners you
can actually talk to: a unit in your classroom, an
onboarding topic at work, a skill your colleagues keep
asking about.

14
00:01:39.381 --> 00:01:46.420
The learner analysis, the task analysis, and the
instructional plan all apply to this one problem.

15
00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:53.244
Choosing something too large, like a course on leadership,
makes every later assignment harder.

16
00:01:53.244 --> 00:01:57.698
Choosing something concrete makes them easier and more
useful.

17
00:01:58.298 --> 00:02:00.955
Each module follows the same pattern.

18
00:02:00.955 --> 00:02:06.198
Read the pages and any linked readings, and take notes in
your own words.

19
00:02:06.198 --> 00:02:11.226
Check your understanding with a short, low-stakes quiz you
can retake.

20
00:02:11.226 --> 00:02:25.518
Post in the discussion by midweek and reply to peers by the
end of the module; the discussions are where the field's
judgment calls get argued out, so treat them as seminar
rather than a box to tick.

21
00:02:25.518 --> 00:02:27.458
And produce the assignment.

22
00:02:27.458 --> 00:02:30.187
Plan on six to eight hours per module.

23
00:02:30.787 --> 00:02:33.774
The course is worth eight hundred points.

24
00:02:33.774 --> 00:02:52.716
The two case analyses are two hundred, the learner and task
analyses one hundred fifty, the instructional plan with its
evaluation and reflection two hundred seventy-five, and the
rest comes from the models assignment, the course
reflection, and participation.

25
00:02:52.716 --> 00:02:56.067
Use APA seventh edition and cite the readings.

26
00:02:56.067 --> 00:03:11.950
You may use generative AI tools to brainstorm, outline, and
check clarity, and you must disclose it; you may not submit
AI-generated text as your analysis or reflection, because
those are the evidence of your thinking.

27
00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:15.957
One forty-eight-hour extension is available on request.

28
00:03:15.957 --> 00:03:22.586
Post logistics questions in the pinned Questions thread, and
email me for anything private.

29
00:03:22.586 --> 00:03:23.898
Let's get started.
